    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Flood damage claims in San Antonio follow different rules than almost any other property claim. The city sits along the Balcones Escarpment in what is often called &ldquo;Flash Flood Alley,&rdquo; where heavy rain falls on thin soils and steep terrain and runs quickly into creeks and streets. The NOAA Storm Events Database lists 420 flash flood reports in Bexar County, second only to hail among event types in the county record. When water rises, the first question is not how much damage there is, but which policy, if any, covers it.</p>
<p>This page explains the difference between flood coverage and homeowners coverage, how flood claims work, and where a public adjuster in San Antonio can and cannot help. For more background, read <a href="/flash-flood-damage-insurance-san-antonio/">flash flood damage and insurance in San Antonio</a>.</p>

<h2>San Antonio Flood History</h2>
<p>Major floods have reached nearly every part of Bexar County:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>October 17, 1998.</strong> NOAA calls it &ldquo;The Great October Flood&rdquo; (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=5672492" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event record</a>). Flooding hit the San Antonio River, the Medina River, and Olmos, Leon, Helotes, Salado, and Cibolo creeks (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=5672615" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI</a>).</li>
<li><strong>Early July 2002.</strong> Days of repeated heavy rain pushed creeks and rivers well above flood stage. The Medina River crested at 63.16 feet at Elmendorf, where flood stage is 35 feet (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=5311464" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI</a>).</li>
<li><strong>June 12, 2025.</strong> Training thunderstorms dropped a swath of 3 to 8 inches of rain across San Antonio. Olmos Creek overflowed, and a news report noted nine homes flooded along Shin Oak Drive in Live Oak (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1272298" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI</a>).</li>
</ul>
<p>More events are listed on our <a href="/storm-history/">Bexar County storm history</a> page.</p>

<h2>Flood Risk Across Greater San Antonio</h2>
<p>Flash flooding here is not limited to riverfront property. On June 12, 2025 alone, NOAA records note 29 flooded low water crossings in northern Bexar County and high water rescues in northwest and north-central San Antonio. Neighborhoods along Salado, Leon, Olmos, and Cibolo creeks, and suburban communities such as <a href="/service-areas/live-oak/">Live Oak</a> and <a href="/service-areas/helotes/">Helotes</a>, have all appeared in the NOAA flood record. Because many homes outside mapped high-risk zones are not required to carry flood insurance, owners are sometimes surprised to learn after a storm that they have no flood coverage at all.</p>
<p>Commercial properties face the same issue. A retail center or warehouse near a creek may have a commercial property policy with a flood exclusion and no separate flood policy, or a flood sublimit far lower than the building value. Review those limits before storm season, not after.</p>

<h2>Flood Policy vs. Homeowners Policy</h2>
<p>Most coverage disputes after heavy rain come down to how water entered the building.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>How the water got in</th><th>Policy that usually responds</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Rising water from a creek, river, or street runoff</td><td>Flood policy (NFIP or private flood); excluded under standard homeowners</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rain through a roof or wall opening created by wind or hail</td><td>Homeowners or commercial property policy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rain through an old roof leak or wear and tear</td><td>Usually not covered by either</td></tr>
<tr><td>Water backing up through a sewer or drain</td><td>Water backup endorsement, if purchased</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sudden burst pipe or appliance leak</td><td>Homeowners or commercial property policy</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Many properties have both kinds of damage after a big storm: a roof opened by wind and a ground floor flooded by rising water. In that case, there may be two separate claims with two separate adjusters. See <a href="/services/storm-damage-claims/">storm damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a>.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Public-Adjuster-Pros-and-Cons-1024x683.webp" alt="Couple meeting with an adjuster at a kitchen table to review a laptop and claim papers" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Reviewing both the flood policy and the homeowners policy side by side helps clarify which damage goes under which claim.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>How NFIP Flood Claims Work</h2>
<p>The National Flood Insurance Program is run by FEMA, and most flood policies in Texas are NFIP policies written through private insurers. A few points shape every NFIP claim:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Waiting period.</strong> New NFIP policies typically take effect 30 days after purchase, with limited exceptions.</li>
<li><strong>Building and contents are separate.</strong> Each has its own limit and deductible, and contents coverage must be purchased separately.</li>
<li><strong>Valuation rules.</strong> Contents are generally paid at actual cash value. Building coverage may be paid at replacement cost only if the property meets specific conditions in the policy.</li>
<li><strong>Proof of loss.</strong> The Standard Flood Insurance Policy sets a strict deadline for a signed proof of loss, generally 60 days after the loss unless FEMA extends it. Missing it can jeopardize the claim.</li>
<li><strong>Documentation.</strong> Adjusters expect high-water-line photos, measurements, and itemized lists of removed materials and contents.</li>
</ul>
<p>Official guidance is available from <a href="https://www.fema.gov/flood-insurance" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FEMA</a> and <a href="https://www.floodsmart.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FloodSmart.gov</a>. Private flood policies follow their own terms, which can differ a lot from NFIP rules.</p>

